{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome has 100% probability), so the reference value is zero, as specified. The reason evaluates each outcome by its difference from this reference (i.e., the outcomes themselves, since reference = 0) and weighs these differences by their probabilities to compute a mean difference for each lottery. For Lottery A: (-4000)*0.25 + (-2000)*0.25 + (0)*0.50 = -1000 - 500 + 0 = -1500. For Lottery B: (-6000)*0.25 + (0)*0.75 = -1500 + 0 = -1500. The mean differences are equal (-1500), so the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
