ut actually, B has a 25 percentage point higher chance of zero than A, not the other way around. So if they are favoring A, their reasoning contradicts the facts. Alternatively, they may be choosing B — but the report doesn't state the choice. We are to ignore the final choice anyway. The key is their deliberation: they state that minimizing the chance of loss was their priority, and they value a higher chance of zero. Their justification is based on probability of avoiding loss, not on outcome magnitudes or deviations from a reference.",
